          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            %em Kochen-Specker theorem
            – developed in 1967 by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simon+Kochen"} Simon Kochen
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ernst+Specker"} Ernst Specker
            – is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/no-go+theorem"} no-go theorem
            that places limits on the types of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hidden+variable+theories"} hidden variable theories
            that may be used to explain the (apparent)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+theory"} probabilistic
            nature of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            in a causal way. It roughly asserts that it is impossible to assign values to all physical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            while simultaneously preserving the functional relations between them. It is a complement to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bell%27s+theorem"} Bell's theorem
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Bell"} John Bell
            in 1964, and is related to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gleason%27s+theorem"} Gleason's theorem
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Gleason57"} Gleason (1957)
            %em Journal of Mathematics and Mechanics
            ). Later in (
            %a{:href => "#ButterfieldHamiltonIsham"}> Butterfield-Hamilton-Isham 98
            ) it was observed that the Kochen-Specker theorem is equivalent to the statement that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+presheaf"} spectral presheaf
            has no
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+elements"} global elements
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            is naturally to be understood as a (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ringed+topos"}> ringed
            )
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr+topos"} Bohr topos
          %h2#kochenspecker_theorem Kochen-Specker theorem
          #Valuation.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℋ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(\mathcal{H})
              be the algebra of
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+operator"} bounded operator
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℋ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℋ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
              is the space of states of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanical
              system, and the elements
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℋ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat A \in B(\mathcal{H})
              represent quantum observables.)
            %p
              A
              %strong valuation
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℋ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(\mathcal{H})
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℋ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\lambda : B(\mathcal{H}) \to \mathbb{R}
            %p
              to the
              = succeed "s," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real number
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %em value rule
                  – the value
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi λ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mover
                          %mi A
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda(\hat{A})
                  belongs to the spectrum of
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mover
                            %mi A
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{A}
              %li
                %p
                  %em functional composition principle
                  (FUNC) – for any pair of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-adjoint+operators"} self-adjoint operators
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mover
                            %mi A
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{A}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi B
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{B}
                  such that
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi B
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                        %mo =
                        %mi h
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mover
                          %mi A
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{B}=h(\hat{A})
                  for some real-valued function
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi h
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
                  we have
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi λ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mover
                            %mi B
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo =
                          %mi h
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi λ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mover
                            %mi A
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda(\hat{B})=h(\lambda(\hat{A}))
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              This has the following equivalent reformulation, which is crucial for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+and+topos+theory"} sheaf-theoretic
              interpretation discussed below.
            %p
              Observed that if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{A}_{1}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{A}_{2}
              commute, then it follows from the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+theorem"} spectral theorem
              that there exists an operator
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{C}
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functions"} continuous functions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h_{1}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h_{2}
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{A}_{1}=h_{1}(\hat{C})
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mover
                          %mi A
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                        %mn 2
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi h
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mover
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{A}_{2}=h_{2}(\hat{C})
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiom
              FUNC in def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Valuation"}
              implies that a valuation satisfies
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                    %mi λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\lambda(\hat{A}_{1} + \hat{A}_{2}) = \lambda(\hat{A}_{1}) + \lambda(\hat{A}_{2})
            %p and
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\lambda(\hat{A}_{1}\hat{A}_{2})=\lambda(\hat{A}_{1})\lambda(\hat{A}_{2})
                    \\,,
            %p
              hence that on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset+of+commutative+subalgebras"} commutative subalgebras
              it is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
            %p
              Therefore a valuation as in def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Valuation"}
              is equivalently a function on the algebra which is an algebra homomorphism on each
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset+of+commutative+subalgebras"} commutative subalgebra
            %p
              (Observe the difference to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-states"} quasi-states
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"}> quantum states
              ), which are positive linear functions on commutative subalgebras, not necessarily respecting the ring structure.)
          %p Now we have:
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              %strong (Kochen-Specker)
            %p
              No valuations on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℋ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(\mathcal{H})
              exist if dim(
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℋ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
              )&gt;2.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              If a valuation
              %em did
              exist and was restricted to a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset+of+commutative+subalgebras"} commutative sub-algebra of operators
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gelfand+spectrum"} Gelfand spectrum
              of the commutative sub-algebra. Since such elements
              %em do
              exist, valuations do exist on any commutative sub-algebra of operators even if not on the whole
              = succeed "-commutative" do
                %em non
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℬ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℋ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{H})
              = succeed "." do
                %em local
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr+topos"} Bohr topos
              of the algebra of observables (see there for more), the local valuations are just the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} internal
              valuations.
          %h2#sheaftheoretic_interpretation Sheaf-theoretic interpretation
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Isham"} Chris Isham
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jeremy+Butterfield"} Jeremy Butterfield
            gave a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theoretic
            reformulation of the Kochen-Specker theorem as follows.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              %strong (category of contexts)
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒱
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℋ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{V}(\mathcal{H})
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              (the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset+of+commutative+subalgebras"} poset of commutative subalgebras
              of the algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℋ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(\mathcal{H})
              of
              = succeed "s)" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+operator"} bounded operator
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"}> object
                  s are
                  %em commutative
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann+algebra"} von Neumann subalgebras
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi V
                          %mo ⊂
                          %mi B
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi ℋ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \subset B(\mathcal{H})
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"}> morphism
                  s
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi V
                          %mn 1
                        %mo →
                        %msub
                          %mi V
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_1 \to V_2
                  are inclusions
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi V
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ⊂
                          %msub
                            %mi V
                            %mn 2
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_1 \subset V_2
          %p
            Isham calls this the
            %strong category of (classical) contexts
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℋ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(\mathcal{H})
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Niels+Bohr"} Niels Bohr
            as being the contexts through which all of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            is to be perceived. (Therefore the word “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr+topos"}> Bohr topos
            ” for the concept that is meant to formalize this perspective of Bohr.)
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              %strong (spectral presheaf)
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝒱
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℬ
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma : \mathcal{V}(\mathcal{B})^{op} \to Set
              be the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
              on the category of contexts such that
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  to
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                        %mo ⊂
                        %mi B
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi ℋ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \subset B(\mathcal{H})
                  it assigned the set underlying the spectrum of
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi V
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                  = succeed "s" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+functional"} linear functional
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi κ
                          %mo :
                          %mi V
                          %mo →
                          %mi ℝ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa : V \to \mathbb{R}
              %li
                %p
                  to an inclusion
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo :
                        %msub
                          %mi V
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ↪
                        %msub
                          %mi V
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i : V_1 \hookrightarrow V_2
                  it assigns the corresponding function
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi i
                          %mo *
                        %mo :
                        %mi Σ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi V
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mi Σ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi V
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i^* : \Sigma(V_2) \to \Sigma(V_1)
                  that sends a functional
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi V
                          %mn 2
                        %mover
                          %mo →
                          %mi κ
                        %mi ℝ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_2 \stackrel{\kappa}{\to} \mathbb{R}
                  to its restriction
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi V
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ↪
                          %msub
                            %mi V
                            %mn 2
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi κ
                          %mi ℝ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_1 \hookrightarrow V_2 \stackrel{\kappa}{\to} \mathbb{R}
            %p
              This is called the
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+presheaf"} spectral presheaf
          %p
            Recall that the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Set
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒱
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi ℋ
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} * = 1_{Set^{\mathcal{V}(\mathcal{H})^{op}}}
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+presheaves"} category of presheaves
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒱
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℋ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{V}(\mathcal{H})
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            that assigns the singleton set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
            (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+element"} global element
            of the spectral presheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            is a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                  %mo :
                  %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e : * \to \Sigma
            in the presheaf topos. Being a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
            of functors, such a global element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Set
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒱
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi ℋ
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %munder
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ̲
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda : 1_{Set^{\mathcal{V}(\mathcal{H})^{op}}} \to \underline{\Sigma}
            of the spectral presheaf, would associate an element of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gelfand+spectrum"} Gelfand spectrum
            of an algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            to that algebra such that all local valuations are global, i.e. for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \subseteq W
            valuations on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            are local valuations on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            but global on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %p
            Because a multiplicative linear functional
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi κ
                  %mo :
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa : V \to\mathbb{R}
            satisfies the axioms of a valuation, def.
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Valuation"}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %p
            By the Kochen-Specker theorem these cannot exist, hence a global element of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            cannot exist. Hence we have:
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p (Hamilton, Isham, Butterfield)
            %p
              The Kochen-Specker theorem is equivalent to the statement that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+presheaf"} spectral presheaf
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              of the algebra of bounded operators has no
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+elements"} global elements
              if the dimension of the Hilbert space is greater than 2.
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#ButterfieldHamiltonIsham"}> Butterfield-Hamilton-Isham 98
            )
          %p
            For more see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr+topos"} Bohr topos
          %h2#related_theorems Related theorems
          %p
            Other theorems about the foundations of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            include:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/order-theoretic+structure+in+quantum+mechanics"} order-theoretic structure in quantum mechanics
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gleason%27s+theorem"} Gleason's theorem
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alfsen-Shultz+theorem"} Alfsen-Shultz theorem
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Harding-D%C3%B6ring-Hamhalter+theorem"} Harding-Döring-Hamhalter theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bell%27s+theorem"} Bell's theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wigner+theorem"} Wigner theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bub-Clifton+theorem"} Bub-Clifton theorem
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/beable"} beable
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+contextuality"} quantum contextuality
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr+topos"} Bohr topos
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interpretation+of+quantum+mechanics"} interpretation of quantum mechanics
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hidden+variable+theory"} hidden variable theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bell%27s+inequalities"} Bell's inequalities
          %p
            Other theorems about the foundations and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interpretation+of+quantum+mechanics"} interpretation of quantum mechanics
            include:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/order-theoretic+structure+in+quantum+mechanics"} order-theoretic structure in quantum mechanics
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alfsen-Shultz+theorem"} Alfsen-Shultz theorem
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Harding-D%C3%B6ring-Hamhalter+theorem"} Harding-Döring-Hamhalter theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fell%27s+theorem"} Fell's theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gleason%27s+theorem"} Gleason's theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wigner+theorem"} Wigner theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bell%27s+theorem"} Bell's theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bub-Clifton+theorem"} Bub-Clifton theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kadison-Singer+problem"} Kadison-Singer problem
          %h2#references References
          %p The original article:
          %ul
            %li#KochenSpecker67
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simon+Kochen"}> Simon Kochen
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ernst+Specker"} Ernst Specker
              = succeed "," do
                %em The problem of hidden variables in quantum mechanics
              %strong 17
              (1967) 59-87 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://www.iumj.indiana.edu/IUMJ/FTDLOAD/1968/17/17004/pdf"}> pdf
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://www.jstor.org/stable/24902153"} jstor:24902153
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-94-010-1795-4_17"} doi:10.1007/978-94-010-1795-4_17
          %p
            Relation to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bell%27s+theorem"} Bell's theorem
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/N.+David+Mermin"}> N. David Mermin
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Hidden Variables and the Two Theorems of John Bell
              %strong 65
              (1993) 803-815 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/RevModPhys.65.803"}> doi:10.1103/RevModPhys.65.803
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.10119"} arXiv:1802.10119
          %p Alternative proofs:
          %ul
            %li
              Del Rajan, Matt Visser,
              %em Kochen-Specker theorem revisited
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1708.01380"}> arXiv:1708.01380
              )
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li
              Costantino Budroni, Adán Cabello, Otfried Gühne, Matthias Kleinmann, Jan-Åke Larsson,
              %em Kochen-Specker Contextuality
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.13036"}> arXiv:2102.13036
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+and+topos+theory"} sheaf-theoretic
            interpretation of the theorem was proposed in
          %ul
            %li#IshamButterfield98
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Isham"}> Chris Isham
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jeremy+Butterfield"} Jeremy Butterfield
              %em A topos perspective on the Kochen-Specker theorem: I. Quantum States as Generalized Valuations
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9803055"}> arXiv:quant-ph/9803055
              )
          %p
            The formulation in terms of presheaves on the category of commutative sub-algebra of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℋ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(\mathcal{H})
            was proposed in part III of
          %ul
            %li#ButterfieldHamiltonIsham
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jeremy+Butterfield"}> Jeremy Butterfield
                , John Hamilton,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Isham"} Chris Isham
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A topos perspective on the Kochen-Specker theorem
              %p
                %em> I. quantum states as generalized valuations
                , Internat. J. Theoret. Phys. 37(11):2669–2733, 1998,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=1669557"} MR2000c:81027
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1023/A:1026680806775"} doi
              %p
                %em II. conceptual aspects and classical analogues
                Int. J. of Theor. Phys. 38(3):827–859, 1999,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=1697983"} MR2000f:81012
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1023/A:1026652817988"} doi
              %p
                %em> III. Von Neumann algebras as the base category
                , Int. J. of Theor. Phys. 39(6):1413–1436, 2000,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9911020"} arXiv:quant-ph/9911020
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=1788498"} MR2001k:81016
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1023/A:1003667607842"} doi
              %p
                %em> IV. Interval valuations
                , Internat. J. Theoret. Phys.
                %strong 41
                (2002), no. 4, 613–639,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=1902067"} MR2003g:81009
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1023/A:1015276209768"} doi
          %p
            The original paper outlining
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bell%27s+theorem"} Bell's theorem
          %ul
            %li
              %span.newWikiWord>
                John S. Bell
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/John+S.+Bell"} ?
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On the Einstein Podolsky Rosen Paradox
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://www.drchinese.com/David/Bell_Compact.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The original paper outlining
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gleason%27s+theorem"} Gleason's theorem
            is
          %ul
            %li#Gleason57
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+M.+Gleason"}> Andrew M. Gleason
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Measures on the closed subspaces of a Hilbert space
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.iumj.indiana.edu/IUMJ/FULLTEXT/1957/6/56050"} web
          %p
            A technical discussion on the interplay of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gleason%27s+theorem"} Gleason's
            and Kochen-Specker theorems and various issues regarding non-contextuality, locality and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bell%27s+inequality"} Bell's inequality
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Valter+Moretti"}> Valter Moretti
              , §5.1.2 of:
              %em Fundamental Mathematical Structures of Quantum Theory: Spectral Theory, Foundational Issues, Symmetries, Algebraic Formulation
              %p
                Springer (2019) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-18346-2"}> doi:10.1007/978-3-030-18346-2
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p The hierarchy of strengths of no-go theorems in quantum context is studied from sheaf theoretic perspective in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Samson+Abramsky"}> Samson Abramsky
              , Adam Brandenburger,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The sheaf-theoretic structure of non-locality and contextuality
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1102.0264"} arxiv/1102.0264
          %p
            Strengthening of the Kochen-Specker theorem and relation to the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+will+theorem"}> free will theorem
            ”:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simon+Kochen"}> Simon Kochen
                ,
                %em On the Free Will Theorem
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.06295"}> arXiv:2207.06295
                \&amp;rbrack;
              %blockquote
                %p The new theorem &amp;lbrack;…&amp;rbrack; does require any use of free will on the experimenter’s part. The theorem also strengthens the Kochen-Specker Theorem
          %p
            Relation of Kochen-Specker to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bell%27s+inequalities"} Bell's inequalities
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Leandro+Aolita"}> Leandro Aolita
              , Rodrigo Gallego, Antonio Acín, Andrea Chiuri, Giuseppe Vallone, Paolo Mataloni, Adán Cabello,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Fully nonlocal quantum correlations
              %strong 85
              032107 (2012) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1105.3598"}> arXiv:1105.3598
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevA.85.032107"} doi:10.1103/PhysRevA.85.032107
        .revisedby
